####################################################################
# cat2b_metric_sensor.rq — CAT2b condition. HAND-AUTHORED.
#
# Answers CQ9 (manuscript Table 1): which alarms coincide with other alarms that
# likely already convey equivalent clinical or technical information?
#
# NL rule (CAT2, "simultaneous alarms likely carrying overlapping or
# redundant information"): "If an alarm conveys the same metric via a
# different sensor, an incoming alarm conveying that metric from another
# sensor can be silenced."
#
# Clause by clause (revised 2026-11-25):
#   "an incoming alarm"        — a PHYSIOLOGICAL alarm, at its arrival, not
#                                one CAT1 has just flagged likely false
#                                positive (see FLAGGED ALARMS below).
#   "an alarm" (active)        — a physiological alarm of the same patient
#                                whose transient graph is in the store now,
#                                other than the incoming one, and not
#                                flagged likely false positive. An active
#                                alarm that is itself silenced by CAT2 still
#                                justifies: it is redundant, not false.
#   "the same metric"          — the same metric type, or the ACTIVE
#                                alarm's metric refines the incoming one's
#                                (rdfs:subClassOf+; revised 2026-11-25, was
#                                either way): a specific active alarm covers
#                                a generic incoming one, not the reverse. An
#                                active ServoU expiratory minute volume
#                                covers an incoming monitor minute volume;
#                                an active monitor minute volume does not
#                                cover an incoming ServoU expiratory one, nor
#                                an unsited SpO2 a pre-ductal one (site
#                                refinement, inference.ttl). Related metrics
#                                of one property are CAT2a's business.
#                                Both metrics must have a metric type: a
#                                blueprint that annotates a metric without
#                                one is minted as plain mda:Metric, which
#                                every metric type refines, so it would
#                                match every metric (added 2026-11-25: one
#                                such blueprint silenced 61 alarm types in
#                                rule_coverage.py). An unknown metric is not
#                                "the same metric" as anything.
#   "via a different sensor"   — the two alarms' pathways share NO sensor.
#                                Two sensors are taken to be the same when
#                                they sit on the same functional unit (the
#                                same particular: patient, device, unit) and
#                                their types are equal or one refines the
#                                other ("some ECG electrode" may be RA).
#                                Anatomical position and laterality play no
#                                part here. A monitor can carry two sensors
#                                of one kind (Tskin and Trect, SpO2 and
#                                SpO2 r), so this treats them as one: it
#                                never silences between two probes on one
#                                functional unit, which is the cautious side.
#                                Where the site changes what is measured, the
#                                metric types differ anyway (site refinement,
#                                revised 2026-11-25). A probe on another
#                                monitor is another sensor (another
#                                functional unit). "No sensor in common"
#                                rather than "some pair differs": an alarm
#                                on two electrodes (ST-II: RA and LL) twice
#                                on one monitor shares both.
#   "overlapping or redundant" — (from the category header, as CAT2a) the
#                                incoming alarm deviates in the SAME
#                                direction and is NOT MORE SEVERE: the
#                                active state is the incoming one or a
#                                refinement of it (mda:refinesConcept, and
#                                skos:broadMatch from the rhythm Absent to
#                                SeverelyDecreased). Two sensors that
#                                disagree (SpO2 low vs high) or an escalation
#                                (low SpO2 -> desaturation) is information.
#                                No priority condition — the NL rule names
#                                none (kept 2026-11-25: unlike CAT2a, a
#                                lower-priority alarm on one device may
#                                silence a higher-priority one on another).
#   "can be silenced"          — for as long as an alarm that justified it
#                                at arrival is still active, shared with
#                                CAT2a: one silence per alarm, lifted when
#                                the last alarm justifying it under EITHER
#                                rule ends (cat2_lift.rq; logged as
#                                cat2_lifted).
#
# FLAGGED ALARMS (agreed 2026-11-25, all rules; as cat2a): an alarm flagged
# likely false positive (CAT1) takes part in no other rule. As the incoming
# alarm it is not also silenced as redundant: "likely false" already
# decides it, and a second verdict would count it twice. As an active alarm
# it justifies no silence: redundancy with an alarm judged false would hide
# the one alarm that may be real. Both flags sit in the flagged alarm's own
# transient graph; CAT1 runs before CAT2 at every arrival, so the incoming
# alarm's flag is already stored, and an active alarm's status is settled
# (a CAT1b withdrawal removes its flag, after which it counts for later
# arrivals).
#
# GRAPHS (contract: structure persists, states are transient). ?gT/?gA:
# the incoming/active alarm's transient graph — its message, the STATE it
# reports on its metric, and any CAT1 flag. ?gP/?gAP: the same alarm's own
# persistent graph — its metric's type and its whole pathway, sensor to
# metric, however many steps (lead derivations, derived metrics). So every
# hop is the alarm's own; none is borrowed from another alarm.
#
# CONTRACT. Bound on entry: ?alarm (the incoming alarm), ?now. Binds:
# ?active (one row per active alarm justifying the silence; all are stored)
# and ?witness (that alarm's transient graph).
# Used by actions/check.rq and actions/silence_insert.rq.
#
# VALIDATION — engine/regression.py: cat2b_pos (low SpO2 on two monitors),
# cat2a_gate (low MAP on two monitors), cat2a_lift_last (low HR on two
# monitors), cat3f and fig2_scenario silence; cat2b_neg, cat2b_opposite and
# cat2b_escalation do not; cat2b_same_sensor never fires CAT2b (forbidden
# firing); cat2a_flagged_justifier and cat2a_flagged_incoming (strict, two
# monitors: a flagged alarm neither justifies nor is silenced, under
# CAT2a or CAT2b); cat2b_lift and cat3f are silenced and lifted when the
# justifying alarm ends; cat2b_untyped_active and cat2b_untyped_incoming
# (strict: an untyped metric, active or incoming, matches nothing);
# cat2b_generic_vs_specific (the direction of "the same metric");
# site_pa_vs_arterial and site_skin_vs_core (site-refined metrics do not
# match their unrelated siblings).
####################################################################

PREFIX mda:      <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX mdapoc:   <https://w3id.org/mda/poc#>
PREFIX rdf:      <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX rdfs:     <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX alarmcat: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/alarm-category/>
PREFIX skos:     <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>

# the incoming alarm: its report (transient), its metric's type (persistent)
GRAPH ?gT { ?alarm mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
            ?alarm mda:hasMessage ?msg . ?msg mda:concernsPatient ?patient .
            ?metric ?stateProp ?state . }
VALUES ?stateProp { mda:hasValueState mda:hasRhythm }
BIND(IRI(CONCAT(STR(?alarm), "#persistent")) AS ?gP)
GRAPH ?gP { ?metric rdf:type ?metricType . }
# not flagged likely false positive (CAT1 has just run on it)
FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH ?gT { ?alarm mdapoc:flaggedLikelyFalsePositive ?incomingFlagEvidence } }

# an active, unflagged alarm of the same patient conveying the same metric
GRAPH ?gA { ?active mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological .
            ?active mda:hasMessage ?activeMsg . ?activeMsg mda:concernsPatient ?patient .
            ?activeMetric ?activeStateProp ?activeState . }
VALUES ?activeStateProp { mda:hasValueState mda:hasRhythm }
FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH ?gA { ?active mdapoc:flaggedLikelyFalsePositive ?activeFlagEvidence } }
BIND(IRI(CONCAT(STR(?active), "#persistent")) AS ?gAP)
GRAPH ?gAP { ?activeMetric rdf:type ?activeMetricType . }
# both typed: plain mda:Metric (a metric annotated without a type) matches nothing
FILTER(?metricType != mda:Metric && ?activeMetricType != mda:Metric)
# equal, or the active metric refines the incoming one (subClassOf* includes
# equality): a specific active alarm covers a generic incoming one, not the
# other way round
?activeMetricType rdfs:subClassOf* ?metricType .
# the same direction, not more severe
?activeState (mda:refinesConcept|skos:broadMatch)* ?state .

# via a different sensor: no sensor behind the active metric can be one behind the incoming metric
FILTER NOT EXISTS {
  GRAPH ?gP  { ?fu mda:hasSensor ?s . ?s rdf:type ?st .
               ?s mda:sensorProducesSignal/mda:analyzedBy/((mda:producesSignal|mda:producesMetric)/mda:analyzedBy)* ?an .
               ?an mda:producesMetric ?metric . }
  GRAPH ?gAP { ?fu mda:hasSensor ?as . ?as rdf:type ?ast .
               ?as mda:sensorProducesSignal/mda:analyzedBy/((mda:producesSignal|mda:producesMetric)/mda:analyzedBy)* ?aan .
               ?aan mda:producesMetric ?activeMetric . }
  { ?st rdfs:subClassOf* ?ast } UNION { ?ast rdfs:subClassOf+ ?st }
}

FILTER(?active != ?alarm)
BIND(?gA AS ?witness)
